# EVERY PATTERN SAYS WHICH OF TWO THINGS IT MEANS.
#
# A leading `/` names ONE place, resolved relative to this file — so it keeps
# meaning the right place if this tree becomes a subdirectory of another. A
# leading `**/` means every depth, on purpose. A pattern with neither behaves
# exactly like `**/` and nobody said so, which is how a rule written for one
# output directory comes to swallow a source directory somebody adds later,
# or another tree's tracked files once the trees are merged.
#
# `.bkp` is anchored for exactly that reason. The reason was measured at the
# assembly: another repository in this workspace TRACKED seven files under a
# `.bkp/` directory, and an every-depth rule here would have taken them out of
# the index the moment the trees became one — silently, because `mv` plus
# `git add -A` exits 0 and leaves `git status` clean.
#
# THOSE SEVEN ARE GONE NOW (the root's clean-up retired them and the root
# ignores `/.bkp/` itself), so the anchoring no longer defends a live file.
# It stays anyway, and not out of caution: `.bkp/` is where this workspace's
# own convention says to put a file before mutating it, so the directory is
# expected to keep reappearing at whichever root someone is working in, and an
# every-depth rule here would reach every one of them. Each root ignores its
# own, which is what makes each root's rule reviewable.

# The merge round's STAGING TREE, and it is declared here because here is
# where it lands. `scripts/harness-root/merge-round.mjs` builds it at
# `resolve(integrationDir, ".staging")`, and the integration directory IS this
# one — so the staging tree is `<this root>/.staging`, never the enclosing
# root's. The enclosing root carried the rule for a while and it defended a
# place nothing writes; anchored patterns resolve relative to the file that
# declares them, so only this file can name the real one.
#
# It is not transient in the way the lock and the report are: `--no-e2e`
# deliberately leaves the staging tree in place for inspection rather than
# deleting it, so the window in which it can be committed is as long as
# somebody's inspection takes.
/.staging/

# Dependencies and build output. Every-depth on purpose: npm creates nested
# `node_modules`, and build output lands beside whatever produced it.
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# A nested `.git` belongs to a vendored checkout, never to this repository.
**/.git/

# Large vendored trees and generated working state, all at this root.
#
# `/large-codebases` carries NO trailing slash, and that is measured rather
# than stylistic: it is a SYMLINK to the sibling tree, and a trailing slash
# makes git match directories only — a symlink is not one. Adding the slash
# for consistency took it out of the ignore set and it appeared as untracked.
# The two below are real directories and keep their slash.
/large-codebases
/babel-work/
/.bkp/

# A merge manifest at a repository ROOT is a stray copy, always.
#
# `scripts/harness-root/merge-round.mjs` reads `<experiments-root>/<expNNN-…>/
# merge-manifest.json` — one level down from the experiments root it is given
# on the command line, never at a repository root — so a manifest sitting HERE
# was copied aside by somebody looking at it and then committed. Three of them
# were (`merge-manifest.json`, `-exp470`, `-exp472`), and five more at the
# other root.
#
# ANCHORED, and that is the whole point of the rule: the every-depth spelling
# would ignore the manifests the merge tool actually reads, which are the one
# input the round cannot run without. The rule names the wrong PLACE for a
# file whose right place is one directory deeper.
/merge-manifest*.json

# `/experiments/` — the 27 files recording this tree's own closed experiment
# rounds lived HERE until the experiments-shape decision (the archive now
# sits at the repository root under `experiments/`, tracked as a record).
# The rule stays: it is anchored to this subtree and keeps any future stray
# `engine/experiments/` from being committed beside the engine.
#
# (The evidence archive `traceability/evidence/` is a tracked record, named by
# no rule: what ships to the product branch is the promotion profile's answer
# — `promotion/testing-to-master.json` excludes it — never an ignore rule. The
# anchored rule and its re-admission that made it "floor bytes" retired with
# the merge door, PROMO-4.)
/experiments/

# Harness runtime state (databases, caches, ledgers). Every-depth: a harness
# run writes one wherever its project root is, including under a fixture.
**/.bridlewise/

# Compiled guardian binary and benchmark run output. Both already name a
# path, so git resolves them relative to this file and they move with it.
guardian/build/
verification/benchmarks/evaluation/runs/

# Python bytecode, which lands beside its source.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo

# SQLite databases and their write-ahead sidecars, wherever a run writes one.
**/*.db
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-wal

# Two TRACKED experiment logs live under scripts/ here. Before the monorepo
# assembly nothing ignored them; the merged root now carries an every-depth
# `**/*.log`, which reached them. They are recorded evidence, not build output,
# so they are named back in one by one — a blanket `!/scripts/**/*.log` would
# also re-admit every future run's output, which the root rule is right about.
!/scripts/exp/exp392-C-test.log
!/scripts/exp463/exp463-scale10.log

# The confinement guard's per-process config, written beside this root for the
# lifetime of one probe (the runner derives the harness root from the config's
# location, so the file cannot live anywhere else) and deleted when the probe
# ends. A whole-tree census that runs mid-probe would otherwise call the file
# undeclared residue. Anchored: the file always lands at exactly this root.
/.confinement-test-*.json
